"executive_summary": "This study investigates the high-energy gamma-ray properties of the Anomalous X-ray Pulsar (AXP) 4U 0142+61 using data from the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope's Large Area Telescope (LAT). AXPs are young, isolated neutron stars with strong magnetic fields, previously known for soft X-ray emission but recently found to emit hard X-rays. The physical origin of this hard emission remains unresolved, making the determination of the spectral energy distribution (SED) peak crucial. The authors performed a detailed search for both persistent and pulsed high-energy gamma-ray emission from 4U 0142+61 using approximately 31.7 megaseconds of LAT data collected between August 2008 and April 2010. To enable a search for pulsed emission, a precise spin ephemeris was derived using contemporaneous Rossi X-ray Timing Explorer (RXTE) observations. The analysis revealed no significant detection of persistent or pulsed high-energy gamma-ray emission. However, 3σ upper limits were established for the persistent flux in the 0.2-1.0 GeV and 1.0-10.0 GeV bands, as well as for the RMS pulsed fraction. These upper limits, when combined with previous hard X-ray data, constrain the spectral break energy to be around 1.1 MeV. This result is consistent with theoretical models such as the coronal emission model by Beloborodov & Thompson (2007) and the quantum electrodynamics model by Heyl & Hernquist (2005a,b), which predict suppression of emission above ~1 MeV due to strong magnetic fields. The study thus provides important constraints on the physical mechanisms driving high-energy emission in magnetars.",
