"executive_summary": "This study presents a sustainable, metal-free photocatalytic oxidative coupling (POC) of arylamines to azoaromatics at charged aqueous interfaces (CAIs) of micelles. The method utilizes Eosin Y (EY) as a low-cost organic photocatalyst and CTAB micelles as a scaffold to facilitate non-diffusive photoinduced electron transfer (PET). Under ambient air atmosphere and green light irradiation, the reaction achieves 100% conversion and selectivity within 4 hours using only 0.6 mol% photocatalyst. This approach significantly outperforms previous methodologies, which often suffered from slow kinetics, poor selectivity, high catalyst doses, or the need for inert atmospheres and organic solvents. The micellar environment modulates redox potentials and brings the photocatalyst and substrates into close proximity, enabling ultrafast PET with a time constant of 0.3 ps. The reaction mechanism involves the oxidation of arylamines by excited EY, followed by radical coupling and regeneration of the photocatalyst via reduction of atmospheric oxygen to hydrogen peroxide. The method demonstrates a broad substrate scope, including electron-rich and electron-deficient arylamines, and offers a green, aqueous-phase alternative for the synthesis of important azoaromatic compounds used in optical storage and molecular switches.",
