{
  "title": "Synthesis, Biological Activity and Molecular Docking of New Tricyclic Series as αglucosidase Inhibitors",
  "authors": "Hatem A Abuelizz; Azman N I Nor; Iwana; Rohaya Ahmad; Anouar El-Hassane; Mohamed Marzouk; Rashad Al-Salahi",
  "field_subfield": "Medicinal Chemistry; Enzyme Inhibition",
  "type_of_paper": "Research Article",
  "executive_summary": "Diabetes mellitus is a prevalent metabolic disorder characterized by hyperglycemia, with global prevalence rising from 4.7% in 1980 to 8.5% in 2014. Current management strategies include α-glucosidase inhibitors like acarbose, which delay carbohydrate hydrolysis to reduce postprandial glucose levels. However, acarbose is associated with significant gastrointestinal side effects, such as flatulence and diarrhea, due to bacterial digestion of undigested carbohydrates in the colon, leading to reduced patient compliance. This study aims to develop novel non-sugar-mimetic α-glucosidase inhibitors by synthesizing 25 tricyclic 2-phenoxypyrido[3,2-e][1,2,4]triazolo[1,5-a]pyrimidin-5(4H)-ones. The synthesis involved modifying well-accepted medicinal chemistry scaffolds, including pyridine, triazole, and pyrimidine, to create hybrids with potential pharmacological activities. In vitro screening revealed that the inhibitory activity of the tested compounds ranged from 30% to 63%. Eight compounds exhibited more than 50% average percent inhibition and were further evaluated for IC50 values. Notably, compounds 6h and 6d demonstrated stronger activity than the reference drug acarbose. Compound 6h exhibited the highest inhibition with an IC50 of 104.07 µM (noted as mM in abstract, likely µM based on context of acarbose comparison, but text says mM in abstract, µM in results section for others, let's check text: \"IC 50 of 104.07 mM\" in abstract, but later \"IC 50 of compound 3 was 167.82 µM\". There is a unit inconsistency in the source text between abstract and results. The results section lists µM for others. I will report the abstract's value as written but note the context). Molecular docking studies indicated that compound 6h forms a stable ligand-α-glucosidase complex with a binding energy of -10.56 kcal/mol, facilitated by hydrogen bonds with Ala234, Arg552, and Asp469, and hydrophobic interactions with Trp329. These findings suggest that the new tricyclic scaffold offers a promising lead for developing clinically effective antidiabetic drugs with potentially fewer gastrointestinal side effects compared to sugar mimetics.",
