"executive_summary": "This study addresses the 'holy grail' challenge of catalytic methane partial oxidation (POM) to methanol and acetic acid under mild, atmospheric pressure conditions. The authors developed a bimetallic Au-Fe catalyst supported on hydrophobic silica (AuFeHS) to achieve high selectivity and yield using hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as the oxidant. The catalyst was synthesized via a modified deposition precipitation method, featuring atomic dispersion of Fe and small Au nanoparticles (1.3-3.0 nm). Characterization techniques including XPS, XANES, and HRTEM confirmed the presence of low-valent Fe species (Fe3-δ+) stabilized by Au, which are critical for methane activation. In batch reactions at 10 bar, the AuFeHS catalyst achieved a methanol yield of 1666 mmol/gFe+Au with 95% selectivity. More significantly, in a continuous flow reactor at atmospheric pressure, the catalyst produced methanol with a yield of 224 mmol/gFe+Au over 4 hours, surpassing previous high-pressure batch reports. The introduction of CO into the feed stream enabled a selectivity switch to acetic acid, yielding 648 µmol/gcat in the continuous process. The hydrophobic nature of the silica support was identified as crucial for maintaining peroxide proximity to active sites and preventing catalyst deactivation. Isotope labeling experiments confirmed that the methanol carbon originated from methane, not the support. The study demonstrates that bimetallic modulation of Fe species by Au, combined with a hydrophobic support, enables efficient methane conversion to value-added oxygenates under benign conditions, offering a pathway toward commercialization of methane-to-oxygenates technologies.",
